        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Siege of Baghdad (1258)</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">The assault began on either 29 or 30 January 1258; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> surrendered on 10 February, the sack began on 13 February and the caliph was executed on 20 February</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> under the prince Hulegu, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and the Seljuk lands and a corps of engineers, an army put at 138,000, nearly 200,000 or 300,000 men by modern scholars; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a>, with around 30,000 troops left in the city, a garrison given elsewhere as at most 50,000 before the sortie of January</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> surrendered, was pillaged and its people killed for about a week, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> was executed. The total of the dead is unknown: later Muslim writers say 800,000 to two million, and Hulegu himself claimed two million.</span></div>
        </div>
