    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">762</span><span class="rf-text">Al-Mansur founds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1253</span><span class="rf-text">Hulegu sets out from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> with the army assigned to him by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1256</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a>' Grand Master surrenders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> follows, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a> refuses to send troops</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1257</span><span class="rf-text">Hulegu begins a correspondence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, who replies defiantly</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1258</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a>'s forces flood the Abbasid camp and rout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s army, and Hulegu reaches the city and invests it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">February 1258</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> breach the walls, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> surrenders after unarmed men who came out are executed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">February 1258</span><span class="rf-text">The sack lasts about a week, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> is executed on 20 February</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1393</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s siege is one of those that led to the city's long-term marginalization, with a second in 1401</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="siege-of-baghdad-1258" data-v="ea6e888f68" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Mongol columns approach Baghdad</b> Hulegu led the main force, with the Golden Horde princes on the right and Kitbuqa on the left. After a council of war in mid-December Baiju returned to the vanguard at Irbil, while Baghdad held a garrison of its own.</li><li><b>Baiju crosses at Mosul and marches south</b> Baiju crossed the Tigris at Mosul with the help of its emir and headed south. He reached the Nahr Isa canal, and his deputy Sughunchaq pushed the advance party to about 40 km from the city.</li><li><b>The dawatdar's army marches out</b> The dawatdar met the advance party with 20,000 infantry and forced it to retreat, and the caliphal army pursued.</li><li><b>The canal dykes are broken and the camp flooded</b> That night Baiju's forces broke the dykes of the Dujayl Canal and flooded the camp of the celebrating Abbasid army. Many drowned.</li><li><b>Baiju's army routs the survivors</b> Baiju's army engaged the remainder the next morning. They were routed, and only a few, the dawatdar among them, got back to Baghdad.</li><li><b>Hulegu and Kitbuqa close in on the city</b> Kitbuqa crossed the Tigris to the south and approached the Karkh suburbs, while Hulegu reached the eastern suburbs. The Mongols then enclosed the whole city with a palisade and a moat, completed within a day.</li><li><b>The first breach is driven back</b> The assault on the walls began on 29 or 30 January. The first breach, in the southeast Ajami tower near Hulegu's camp, was made on 1 February, but the Mongols were driven back.</li><li><b>The Mongols take the east battlements</b> Further breaches over the next two days let the Mongols reach the east battlements, and they seized control of them by 4 February.</li><li><b>The dawatdar tries to escape by river</b> The dawatdar sailed down the Tigris to escape. The Mongol preparations on the river held and forced him back into the city with a loss of three ships.</li><li><b>Unarmed men who came out are executed</b> A large number of unarmed soldiers and inhabitants came out of the city, apparently hoping to be spared and allowed to settle in Syria. They were divided into groups and executed.</li><li><b>The caliph surrenders the city</b> After envoys had failed to win terms, the caliph surrendered the city on 10 February, bringing his family and 3,000 dignitaries.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mongke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongke.jpg" alt="Möngke" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%B6ngke_enthroned._Tarikh-i_Jahangushay-i_Juvaini,_1438,_Shiraz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1438 artist, Shiraz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> was founded in 762 by al-Mansur, the second Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, who wanted a capital away from threats and near the dynasty's power base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Wealthy from the trade routes and taxes it controlled, it became a world city and the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Islamic Golden Age</a>, with centres of learning such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a>. In the tenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> lost power; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> occupied Baghdad in 945 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> in 1055, when the caliphs held only local authority. The caliphate recovered under al-Nasir (r. 1180–1225).</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> reached the region after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>. From 1230 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chormaqan">Chormaqan</a> established Mongol hegemony in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasus</a>. They besieged Irbil in 1237 and raided up to Baghdad's walls the next year, but Baghdad remained unconquered and even defeated a Mongol force in 1245.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a> became khan in 1251, he sent his brother Hulegu westwards to destroy the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismaili-state">Nizari Ismaili</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a> and secure the submission of the Abbasid caliphs. Hulegu had expected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a> to supply troops against the Assassins. The caliph first agreed, but his ministers argued that the request was meant to empty Baghdad of defenders, and he refused. The Assassins' strongholds fell in late 1256.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-mustasim.jpg" alt="al-Musta'sim" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinar_Abbasside_-_al-Musta%27sim_bi-llah_-_641_AH_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DrFO.Jr.Tn, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> assigned Hulegu one fifth of the empire's manpower, a figure modern scholars have calculated as 138,000, nearly 200,000 or 300,000 men. The army included troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> under King Hetoum I, a thousand-strong corps of military engineers led by Guo Kan, auxiliaries from across the empire and generals from all branches of the Mongol imperial family, among them three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> princes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a> brought Seljuk, Georgian and Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> to join it in the summer of 1257.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> was left with around 30,000 troops. Another figure puts the garrison at no more than 50,000 before the sortie of January, untrained and largely incapable. The walls, weakened by a flood and in disrepair, were in poor shape, and in 1256 sectarian violence between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a> and Shi'a had broken out after the flood. Later Sunni writers accused the Shi'ite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Muhammad ibn al-Alqami of betraying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> through secret talks with Hulegu.</p><p>In their exchange of letters, Hulegu demanded that the caliph submit and send his vizier, commander of the soldiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawatdar">dawatdar</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">Al-Musta'sim</a> called Hulegu young and ignorant and said he could summon armies from all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was hostile to him and the Ayyubid rulers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> were concerned with their own survival. Envoys of Hulegu were mocked by mobs in Baghdad's streets.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hulegu sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a> on 6 December 1257, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> princes on his right wing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> on the left in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzistan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> with help from its emir and reached the Nahr Isa canal in mid-January 1258. On 16 January the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawatdar">dawatdar</a> with 20,000 infantry forced his deputy Sughunchaq to retreat. That night Baiju's forces broke the dykes of the Dujayl Canal and flooded the camp of the celebrating Abbasid army. Many drowned, and the rest were routed the next morning.</p><p>Hulegu reached the eastern suburbs on 22 January, where he was welcomed by local Shi'ites. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> erected a palisade around the city and dug a moat inside it, finishing both within a day, built mounds for mangonels and ballistae, and used burning naphtha. Pontoon bridges across the Tigris stopped any escape by river. The assault began on 29 or 30 January. Messages guaranteeing safety, including to Christians, were shot into the city by arrow. The first breach, in the Ajami tower on 1 February, was driven back, but further breaches let the Mongols take the east battlements by 4 February. The dawatdar tried to flee by river and was forced back with the loss of three ships.</p><p>Envoys, among them al-Alqami and the Patriarch Makkikha II, were sent during the next week. Hulegu insisted on unconditional surrender, especially after one of his commanders was wounded by an arrow during a parley. On 7 February a large number of unarmed soldiers and inhabitants came out, apparently hoping to be spared, and were divided into groups and executed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> surrendered on 10 February with his family and 3,000 dignitaries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis IX of France" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saintlouis_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hulegu asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> to order the population to leave the city after laying down their weapons, and those who obeyed were slaughtered. The sack began on 13 February. Sayyids, scholars, merchants who traded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, and Christians, for whom Hulegu's Christian wife Doquz Khatun had interceded, were told to mark their doors so their houses would be spared. The rest of the city was pillaged and its people killed for a full week. Kirakos Gandzaketsi, a 13th-century Armenian historian, says the Christians in Hulegu's army took special pleasure in it.</p><p>How many inhabitants were killed is unknown. Later Muslim writers estimated between 800,000 and two million, and Hulegu told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> that his army had killed two million, a count described as obviously inflated. An epidemic among the survivors, which scholars have debated as possibly plague, may have raised the figures. A physician who was present recorded that a pestilence killed so many refugees that their bodies could not be buried and were thrown into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>.</p><p>On 15 February Hulegu made the caliph reveal his treasures, most of which were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> or to Shahi Island, and gave the palace to Makkikha as a church. On 20 February, after the killing and plundering had stopped, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a> was executed with his family and court. Al-Alqami was kept as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and died less than three months later. Hulegu stationed 3,000 soldiers in the city, ordered it rebuilt and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> reopened, and left on 8 March.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-din-al-tusi">Nasir al-Din al-Tusi</a>, who was likely present, recorded the exchange at Hulegu's banquet, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, offered a golden tray and told to eat, said it was not edible and that his fate was God's will. A physician who was present recorded the pestilence among the refugees. Kirakos Gandzaketsi, an Armenian historian of the 13th century, wrote that the Christians in Hulegu's army took special pleasure in the sack, and Hulegu's own letter to Louis IX gives his claim of two million dead. The historian René Grousset called the exchange of letters with the caliph one of the most magnificent dialogues in history.</p><p>Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> writers accused al-Alqami of treachery, but Mongol policy was to execute traitors, and Hulegu instead reappointed him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> and other Christian writers tell that the caliph was starved to death among his treasures, a folktale likely drawn from the banquet. A 16th-century historian wrote that so many books were thrown into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> that its colour turned black. The start of the assault, 29 or 30 January, is uncertain, and so are the size of the army, the garrison, the deaths and whether plague was the epidemic.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> ended the 500-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> there. A member of the dynasty reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> installed him as al-Mustansir II, but he and his descendants were puppets of the Mamluk state and gained little recognition in the wider Muslim world; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> later usurped them and kept the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> into the 20th century. Power shifted from Baghdad to cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> that Hulegu founded after the siege.</p><p>Muslim writers have traditionally blamed this one event for the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Islamic Golden Age</a> and, consequently, the later rise of the West, an argument modern historians have criticised as simplistic and lazy. The historian Michal Biran has shown that large libraries reopened for learning and teaching within two years of the siege. Baghdad prospered under Hulegu's Ilkhanate, though it declined compared with Tabriz, and Hulegu and his successors patronized musical and literary traditions.</p><p>According to Biran, it was later sieges, those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1393 and 1401 and of the Ottomans in 1534, that ensured the city's long-term marginalization. The sack has been described as a calculated decision to show the consequences of defying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> rather than wanton destruction, and Hulegu is said to have executed the caliph to break the myth of the caliphate as an all-powerful, invulnerable and inviolate entity.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>In early 1258 a Mongol army under Hulegu invested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, breached its walls within days and took the city on 10 February. A week of pillaging and killing followed, with the dead put at 800,000 to two million by later writers, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a> was executed.</em></p>

